+\section{Problem Definition}\label{sec:problem}
+\subsection{Problem State and Dynamics}
+Let $\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})$ be a complete graph with depot
+$v_0$ and bins $\mathcal{V}\setminus\{v_0\}$. Bin $i$ has location
+$\vec{l}_i\in\mathbb{R}^2$, capacity $C_i$, and waste level
+$w_{i,d}\in[0,C_i]$ on day $d$. The system state is
+$\vec{w}_d={[w_{1,d},\ldots,w_{V,d}]}^\top$.
+
+Daily accumulation $\delta_{i,d}\sim P_i$ is bin-specific and stochastic. If
+bin $i$ is collected on day $d$, the next day's level contains only the next
+increment; otherwise accumulation continues to capacity:
+\begin{equation}
+    w_{i, d+1} =
+    \begin{cases}
+        \delta_{i, d+1} & \text{if } v_i \in \mathcal{A}_d \\
+        \min(w_{i, d} + \delta_{i, d+1}, C_i) & \text{if } v_i \notin \mathcal{A}_d
+    \end{cases}
+\end{equation}
+
+\subsection{Optimization Objective}
+
+On day $d$, the policy produces a set of $K_d$ routes. The route count is not
+fixed in advance: it is whatever the policy needs to serve its chosen bins under
+the capacity constraint below. Route $k$ visits $T_{d,k}$ bins and starts and
+ends at $v_0$:
+\begin{equation}
+    \mathcal{A}_{d,k}=(a_{0,k},a_{1,k},\ldots,a_{T_{d,k},k},a_{T_{d,k}+1,k}),
+    \qquad a_{0,k}=a_{T_{d,k}+1,k}=v_0.
+\end{equation}
+
+Let $\mathcal{A}_{d,\cdot}$ denote the union of non-depot visits. The daily
+problem is
+
+\begin{equation}
+\begin{aligned}
+    \textrm{maximize} \quad & \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{A}_d, \bm{w}_d) = r_w \sum_{v_i\in\mathcal{A}_{d,\cdot}}w_{i,d}
+    -c_{km}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\sum_{t=0}^{T_{d,k}}
+    \dist(a_{t,k},a_{t+1,k}) \label{eq:profit_function}\\
+    \textrm{subject to} \quad
+    & a_{t,k}\neq a_{t',k'} \quad
+      \forall (t,k)\neq(t',k'),\ 1\leq t\leq T_{d,k},\ 1\leq t'\leq T_{d,k'},\\
+    & \sum_{t=1}^{T_{d,k}}w_{a_{t,k},d}\leq Q
+      \quad \forall k\in\{1,\ldots,K_d\}.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+Here $r_w$ is revenue per kilogram, $c_{km}$ is travel cost per kilometer, and $Q$
+is the per-route payload capacity. Each route is one capacity-feasible depot-to-depot
+trip; the formulation bounds the load of every route but does not bound how many
+routes a day may contain, so $K_d$ is an outcome of the daily decision rather than a
+parameter of it. Sect.~\ref{sec:protocol} states how this was configured in the
+reported experiment. Optional service distinguishes the VRPP from the classical
+VRP\@: the policy chooses a profitable subset as well as its routes~\cite{Archetti2014VRPProfits}.
+Eq.~\eqref{eq:profit_function} contains no term penalizing an unvisited bin that
+overflows: a distant bin's marginal revenue $r_w w_{i,d}$ can be lower than the
+detour cost required to reach it, in which case a route constructor solving this
+objective alone has no incentive to serve it. The only mechanism that forces a
+critically full bin into a route is the upstream mandatory set $\mathcal{M}_d$
+Sect.~\ref{sec:methodology}; Sect.~\ref{sec:discussion} returns to this point as
+the structural reason selection strategy, not route construction, sets the bulk of
+the observed overflow-service range.
+
+Because each collection changes the next state, policy $\bm{\pi}$ maximizes
+expected cumulative profit over the horizon:
+\begin{equation}
+    \max_{\bm{\pi}} \mathbb{E} \left[ \sum_{d=1}^D \mathcal{P}_d(\mathcal{A}_{d,\cdot}, \bm{w}_d) \right],
+\end{equation}
+where $\mathcal{P}_d$ is defined in~\eqref{eq:profit_function}.
+
